<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Icaria</h1><p class="st-meta">1912 CE – 1912 CE · capital Agios Kirykos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny independent republic declared on the Aegean island of Icaria in 1912 after islanders expelled the Ottoman garrison, lasting months until union with Greece.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikaria">Icaria</a>, a mountainous island west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-samos">Samos</a>, was under Ottoman rule when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy's</a> war with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> weakened Turkish control in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. On 17 July 1912 the islanders, led by the doctor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-malachias">Ioannis Malachias</a>, rose against the small Ottoman garrison, forced its surrender and proclaimed the Free State of Icaria.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The new state set up a provisional government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agios-kirykos">Agios Kirykos</a>, adopted its own flag and issued postage stamps. It lacked money, an army and any outside recognition, and the islanders, who lived by farming, herding and emigration, hoped for annexation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> rather than lasting independence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> began, the Greek navy arrived in November 1912 and the island joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece</a>. The episode lasted about five months and is still commemorated on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikaria">Icaria</a> every July.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1912 CE – 1912 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.75%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1912 CE – 1912 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Agios Kirykos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ioannis Malachias</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Independent State of Icaria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1912&amp;focus=free-state-of-icaria" aria-label="Free State of Icaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5337 825 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State of Icaria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5657 1081L5709 970M5657 1081L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5657 1081h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5657 1081h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Agios Kirykos. <a href="/atlas?year=1912&amp;focus=free-state-of-icaria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
